01.a|ABFJ|Add a variable-length byte-key comparator to the retained stable insertion sort. Count comparator calls and inspected key bytes separately for [aa,ab,a] with explicit lengths; specify prefix ordering, failure admission and output permutation.|One comparison is assumed constant when key lengths vary.|Ordering is a,aa,ab; the byte-work bound includes key lengths and the stable identity sequence, not merely record count.|Transfer to graph edges with variable-length labels and output-sensitive matching.|Separate the comparison interface from its internal scan.|A record-count bound requires a fixed-cost comparator assumption. Shared prefixes change byte work without changing the number of comparisons; instrumentation must preserve the comparator result and stability.
01.b|BCDFJ|Instrument the accepted insertion kernel at comparison, shift and placement events, then enumerate all arrays of length0..6 over keys0..2 with identity tags. Compare shifts with independently enumerated inverted index pairs and detect counter overflow before increment.|A counter added at the wrong source line measures loop tests or failed reads instead of semantic work.|All shifts equal inversion counts; equal-key identities preserve order; empty/singleton states perform zero placements. An exhausted counter reports unavailable without a plausible wrapped count.|Replace insertion with binary insertion and derive which count identity survives.|An unsuccessful final comparison still counts.|The inversion certificate is independent of the insertion loop. Binary search reduces comparisons but cannot eliminate required record movement under this representation.
01.c|ADHIJ|For one accepted compiled kernel, make three ledgers: semantic events, an actually executed instruction path, and the static connected CFG. Find two inputs sharing a CFG but differing in loop iterations and explain each observation boundary.|A disassembly size or cyclomatic number is labeled executed instructions.|Static graph and code size stay fixed while dynamic counts differ; retired instruction and cycle evidence require their own target observations.|Enable inlining and explain how function boundaries and counts change without changing the source contract.|A listing is a set of instructions, not a path.|Optimization can remove source operations or duplicate instructions; all three models need a named build and input. An emulator path is functional evidence, not silicon timing.
02.a|ABFJ|Prove bounds for f(n,m)=3n*m+5n+7 for integers n,m>=1, giving positive constants and thresholds; explain why holding m fixed changes the one-parameter statement. Check boundary arithmetic independently.|Deleting terms is treated as a quantified proof without a parameter domain.|3nm<=f<=15nm for n,m>=1; fixed positive m yields Theta(n), while m=n yields Theta(n squared).|Allow m=0 and derive a valid two-parameter expression including n+1.|Use n<=nm only after stating m>=1.|The original bound fails to provide a nonzero upper bound at m=0. A corrected all-domain expression separates the degenerate input, rather than hiding it in measured samples.
02.b|ABFHJ|Extend the retained traversal certificate to a graph with parallel edges and repeated emitted matches. Define V,E,Z and count visit, edge inspection and output operations; exhibit a tiny graph whose Z exceeds V.|Output work and representation-dependent edge multiplicity disappear from the bound.|The certificate includes Z and stored edges, and distinguishes worst-case over inputs from a bound for each trace.|Change output from materialized records to an interruptible streaming sink with partial progress.|An edge set and an adjacency list need not encode the same multiplicities.|The sink adds a failure and backpressure contract. Counting only accepted outputs must not erase attempted output work or repeated retries.
02.c|AFHIJ|Construct two nonnegative cost functions agreeing on every sampled n<=1024 but with different eventual growth, and two algorithms whose asymptotic order differs from their measured ordering within that interval.|Finite slope evidence establishes an asymptotic theorem or an all-size speed ranking.|Give explicit piecewise definitions, a divergent witness beyond the sample set and a crossover calculation under stated constants.|Require monotone functions and preserve indistinguishability on the measured interval.|Use a threshold outside the observed set.|Monotonicity does not recover the unobserved tail. Measurement supports the sampled regime; asymptotic quantifiers need a model proof.
03.a|BCHJ|Trace alternating growth and failed allocation in the accepted table. Keep successful append costs, failed-attempt work and maximum single-operation cost in separate columns; include a nonempty initial table.|The successful expansion amortization silently bounds arbitrarily many failed retries.|The original successful-prefix bound holds only for its admitted operations; repeated failure adds separately bounded attempt cost and no ownership change.|Introduce deletion with shrink at half capacity and find a grow/shrink thrashing trace.|A failed acquisition is still an attempted operation.|The usual expansion-only potential cannot cover an arbitrary shrink policy. A hysteresis threshold or revised charging scheme is needed, with the initial state stated.
03.b|ABCFJ|For the nonempty table live=5,capacity=8, retain Phi=2*live-capacity and derive every prefix through the next growth. Test a proposed certificate that drops Phi0; separately evaluate live=1,capacity=8.|An empty-state proof is reused for a negative or nonzero initial potential.|Telescoping always includes Phi0; a negative initial potential requires an offset or a restricted reachable-state argument before deriving an upper bound.|Construct a piecewise potential supporting expansion and quarter-full shrinking.|Check the sign used when converting the identity into an inequality.|A telescoping identity alone is not a bound. Nonnegative final potential and an accounted initial potential determine whether actual work is bounded by charged work.
03.c|AHIJ|Replace fixed-cost element copies with records owning variable-size payloads and a fallible deep-copy operation. Identify each assumption of the retained doubling proof that changes and derive a byte-weighted sequence bound.|Amortized constant insertion implies bounded allocator latency, destructor time or response time.|The claim is deterministic over the declared sequence but depends on copy weights; worst single growth and allocator latency remain separate.|Use move-only logical ownership with a constant-size handle and compare rollback requirements.|Count what is copied, not merely how many entries exist.|A handle move can preserve constant per-element cost if ownership rules permit it; ISO C does not enforce that logical move or prevent duplicate destruction.
04.a|ADHIJ|Choose a branching lower-bound search path with a duplicate-key boundary. Record taken instructions from the actual trace and classify loads, branches and arithmetic under a declared weighted model; reject the alternative of counting every disassembled instruction once.|Static instruction listings supply an executed mix.|The trace reaches the required first matching index; the mix includes repeated and untaken paths correctly and is tied to its binary.|Change branch direction without changing final index and compare mix and prediction.|Reconcile each loop iteration with the control-flow edge.|An instruction count is still independent of microarchitectural overlap, stalls and speculation; model CPI weights are assumptions, not measured properties.
04.b|AFHIJ|Derive a speedup bound when40 percent of baseline time is improved twofold, then include a new serialization overhead of10 percent of original time. Use the parent260-cycle model only as a model and compare an actual changed working set separately.|An isolated kernel speedup transfers unchanged to end-to-end latency.|Without added overhead the bound is1.25; with0.1 original-time overhead it is1/0.9. Actual residuals remain observations, not corrections chosen to fit the model.|Overlap the improved phase with an unchanged phase and rederive the critical path.|Use the original total as the denominator for each fraction.|Amdahl assumptions include fixed work and additive nonoverlapping phases. Overlap changes the composition equation and can make a local improvement invisible.
04.c|BCFHIJ|Construct two connected CFGs with equal cyclomatic complexity but different nested-loop work; emit graph validation that rejects disconnected nodes and duplicate edges. Record code bytes independently from either graph metric.|Equal graph complexity means equal path feasibility or runtime cost.|Both metrics use an explicit entry/exit convention; equal values coexist with linear versus quadratic body counts, and no infeasible path is claimed executed.|Add an early exit that changes feasible paths while keeping the graph metric unchanged.|Graph edges are structural possibilities.|A basis of graph paths is a test-design aid. Predicate correlations can make paths infeasible, and optimization can change the graph representation.
05.a|BCFHJ|Extend the lifetime fixture with two borrowed views of work and a scratch interval ending exactly when result begins. Implement a sweep over event times and compute live, capacity, retained and slack peaks without counting views as owners.|Adding separate category maxima or aliases gives a simultaneous memory peak.|Half-open intervals avoid overlap at the shared endpoint; each owned allocation is counted once; category maxima retain their own timestamps.|Transfer an owner between components at the same timestamp and preserve global accounting.|Assign allocation identities before sorting events.|A local ownership transfer changes attribution without creating or destroying storage. Deterministic same-time event semantics are necessary for a reproducible ledger.
05.b|ACDHIJ|Parse actual static, dynamic,bounded and unbounded .su records from the selected build. Add one unknown indirect callee and a mutually exclusive branch; compute only feasible known call-chain sums and return unknown for the unresolved path.|Maximum observed watermark or sum of all frame maxima proves a whole-program bound.|Known paths have conditional sums; unknown/dynamic-unbounded paths cannot be given a finite certified maximum. Compiler/build identities stay attached to records.|Enable LTO and rebuild the call graph before combining frame data.|Different compiler outputs cannot be mixed into one executable model.|Inlining and tail calls alter simultaneous frames. ABI stack alignment, callbacks and interrupt nesting must be included or explicitly unresolved.
05.c|AFHIJ|For a bounded parsing pipeline, report symbolic O(n) payload, actual reserved bytes, allocator overhead, resident pages and sampled stack separately. Explain a run where freed payload falls but RSS stays high.|Live heap and operating-system resident accounting are equivalent.|Each quantity has its own scope and units; retained allocator pages can explain the discrepancy without proving a leak.|Move scratch from heap to a bounded stack frame and reassess the requirement.|Follow ownership and page retention as different ledgers.|Reducing one category can increase another. A useful decision names which limit is binding rather than collapsing all bytes into one incomparable total.
06.a|BCFHJ|Implement the parent's two-block LRU write-back adapter and compare every reference sequence of length<=5 over blocks0..2 and read/write actions with a simple independent list model. Keep CPU cache fixtures out of this policy.|A CPU cache miss and a disk buffer transfer are the same event.|Hits update recency; dirty eviction and final flush each write once; geometry and index rejection preserve state.|Replace LRU with FIFO and construct a sequence that distinguishes them.|A hit changes LRU recency but not FIFO insertion order.|The independent model must encode the same write-allocation and final-flush rules. An alternative policy requires new expected histories, not renaming the old result.
06.b|ACFHJ|Add write-only, repeated-read and empty streams to the traffic ledger. Preserve read, written and useful byte totals as integers and represent an undefined amplification ratio explicitly.|Amplification is always at least one or zero useful work gives zero amplification.|Repeated warm reads can yield a ratio below one under this definition; useful=0 has no ratio; bytes are counted at the named buffer boundary.|Use compressed blocks and distinguish logical useful bytes from encoded transfer bytes.|State the numerator and denominator before comparing ratios.|Amplification is a defined accounting ratio, not an intrinsic device constant. Compression and repeated use change the relation without arithmetic failure.
06.c|DFHIJ|Run the same disposable-file read twice through the accepted adapter, retaining accepted-byte/syscall observations and permitted device accounting. Predict cache/readahead/shared-work hypotheses before collecting data.|Unchanged device counters mean no useful reads or a syscall count identifies physical transfers.|Logical output must match in both runs; unavailable/shared device attribution stays explicit. Warmth is inferred only within the observed setup.|Repeat after an unrelated local process accesses the same file and assess attribution limits.|A page-cache hit still satisfies the application read.|No global cache flush or privilege change is required. A model transfer count is not promoted to an actual bus trace.
07.a|ACFGJ|Instrument submit, caller return, completion, callback return and release for the retained async adapter. Include completion-before-submit-return and cancellation-request-without-quiescence schedules.|A completion notification or timeout alone always permits freeing all request state.|Release follows the final accessor boundary, which may include callback return and caller return; cancellation intent does not establish quiescence.|Permit a callback to enqueue a successor using the same owner and derive the new lifetime interval.|List every actor that can still access the object.|The maximum of two timestamps is sufficient only for the parent two-actor model. Adding callbacks introduces a distinct access interval that must be closed.
07.b|ABFHJ|Compute latency, throughput and occupied-time union for three overlapping requests, including a failed completion excluded from successful-item throughput. Publish the exact observation window and censoring rule.|Summing inclusive spans or dividing submissions by call time measures completed throughput.|Union time never exceeds the enclosing window; throughput counts the declared completed class and excludes or separately reports censored operations.|Use a sliding observation window containing requests submitted earlier.|An operation can cross either window boundary.|Cohort latency and window throughput use different populations. Boundary censoring must not silently turn unfinished operations into fast successes.
07.c|AFHIJ|Measure process and per-thread CPU time around two worker threads plus a blocking wait. Compare their scopes with elapsed time and explain a negative elapsed-minus-process-CPU value without calling it negative waiting.|CPU time is bounded by wall time for a multithreaded process.|Process CPU may exceed elapsed; wall-minus-CPU is not a universal wait metric and per-thread measurement excludes other threads.|Include a child process and determine which CPU accounting clocks change.|Identify the set of execution contexts charged by each clock.|Accounting aggregation and overlap explain the sign. Different APIs may include children or use different units; their contracts must be checked.
08.a|BCDGJ|Inject positive partial progress, EINTR, EAGAIN, zero and terminal EIO at each cursor boundary of the retained eight-byte write. Track attempt count and accepted prefix; reject impossible over-reported counts before arithmetic.|Restarting after an error is harmless because the call failed.|Accepted bytes are never duplicated; only positive results advance the cursor; retry budget and zero-progress termination are explicit.|Add a vectored write spanning two buffer boundaries.|Use a total accepted-byte cursor before deriving vector/index offsets.|A later error does not roll back earlier accepted bytes. The multi-buffer cursor needs checked cumulative lengths and unchanged borrowed lifetimes.
08.b|BCGHIJ|Collect separate buffered-write and requested-file-sync observations in a disposable directory, including an injected sync error. Retain file creation/close lifetime and distinguish requested durability from a crash experiment.|A completed write or matching file length establishes durable publication.|The sync failure remains a failure despite readable bytes; timing phases and exact status are separate; no power-loss result is inferred.|Publish by temporary file plus rename and identify the additional directory synchronization boundary.|Name which object each synchronization operation covers.|A file-content boundary and a directory-entry boundary are distinct. A model of atomic rename does not automatically establish persistence after power loss.
08.c|AFHIJ|Reconcile eight accepted bytes, logical file size and a shared4096-byte device observation. Include sparse-file allocation and delayed writeback as competing explanations; state what further measurement distinguishes them.|All device traffic observed during the interval belongs to this component.|The ratio512 describes the captured scope only; attribution and allocation granularity remain unresolved without additional evidence.|Compare a preallocated file with a newly created sparse file under equal byte correctness.|Separate logical extent from allocated blocks and observed traffic.|Shared counters permit only scoped inference. Equal file bytes do not imply equal metadata or physical I/O work.
09.a|BCFIJ|Implement checked timespec subtraction and nanosecond conversion with a declared maximum duration. Handle borrow across seconds, equal timestamps, reversed inputs and overflow; record actual clock identity and getres result.|Nanosecond fields imply nanosecond accuracy or overflow-free conversion.|Equal reads are valid; reversed/overflowed intervals reject; resolution, accuracy and bracket cost remain distinct.|Use a wrapping hardware counter with an independently justified less-than-one-wrap bound.|Normalize the subsecond borrow before multiplication.|A host clock and a modular counter need different admissibility proofs. The observed difference cannot itself prove the interval bound.
09.b|BFHIJ|Extend the paired harness with31 raw pairs, randomized or alternating order, warmup and a consumed checksum. Compare median paired difference with difference of medians; preserve the pairing before sorting summaries.|Sorting each list first preserves paired treatment effects.|Both statistics are reported under their own definitions; pair identities, setup exclusions and overhead sensitivity remain reproducible.|Introduce a drift trend and compare blocked versus alternating trial order.|Pairing is information that sorting can destroy.|A deterministic order can confound treatment and drift. Experimental design and raw observations are part of the evidence, not optional presentation detail.
09.c|AFHJ|Create a tail report with an explicit nearest-rank rule, strict deadline relation and retained outliers. Add one delayed trial and show how the estimated tail changes; distinguish observed maximum from an asserted WCET.|The largest sampled value proves a deadline bound.|Report sample size, misses and sensitivity; no finite maximum is relabeled a universal guarantee.|Introduce censored timeout trials and derive a report that cannot hide them as missing data.|Timeout is an observation with a lower bound, not a zero duration.|Removing censored failures biases latency evidence. A research conclusion can remain inconclusive when the available experiment cannot establish the required guarantee.
10.a|ADHIJ|Collect one permitted or denied per-process PMU attempt for the reused kernel and retain CPU, kernel, event, scope and binary identities. Classify unavailable, unsupported and observed-zero results separately.|A denied counter is encoded as zero work.|Every result includes status and scope; no host permission setting changes and no unrelated process is observed.|Move the same request to another CPU model and identify event meanings requiring new authority.|A numeric zero is meaningful only after a successful observation.|Generalized event names do not ensure identical microarchitectural coverage. A denied run supports only an availability conclusion.
10.b|ABCFJ|Implement checked grouped-counter decoding for raw1200/3000, enabled20,running10, then test zero-running, running>enabled, mixed group IDs and arithmetic overflow. Preserve raw counts alongside scaled rational estimates.|Scaling reconstructs exact unobserved counts or permits ratios across unrelated groups.|The valid estimates are2400/6000 with CPI2.5; zero-running and invalid groups have no justified ratio; overflow rejects rather than wraps.|Use two nonstationary phases with identical aggregate running fraction but different sampled phases.|Co-scheduled identity matters beyond numeric duration equality.|Scaling assumes representativeness. Different sampled phases can yield different estimates despite identical enabled/running totals.
10.c|ADHIJ|Compare the work contract with available instruction/branch/cache events or explicitly unavailable evidence. Give two hypotheses for equal instructions but different cycles and choose a discriminating additional observation.|A single event count identifies the bottleneck causally.|Frequency and memory-stall hypotheses require distinct evidence; sample attribution is not automatically ownership or exact instruction location.|Change branch predictability while keeping semantic work fixed.|Predict how each hypothesis changes more than one observable.|A plausible narrative after measurement is weaker than a predeclared discriminating experiment. Unsupported events leave the corresponding explanation open.
11.a|ACFIJ|Write a read-only inventory/parser for a captured or permitted cgroupv2 hierarchy. Classify cpu.max, cpu.stat, memory.current, memory.peak, io.stat and ancestor policy; preserve absent controllers as unknown.|A visible control file implies delegated permission or isolated ownership.|No settings are written; policy and observation have separate fields; hierarchy and descendants are part of the scope.|Move the workload into an already prepared delegated subtree in a separate authorized experiment.|Availability, delegation and attribution are different questions.|This authoring task grants no host-policy changes. A future prepared exercise must preserve the original state and record inherited constraints.
11.b|BCDFJ|Parse keyed snapshots with reordered fields, new optional keys, a decreasing gauge and a reset cumulative counter. Derive per-device deltas and refuse a fresh-window memory peak without the required same-descriptor reset history.|Every decreasing field is invalid or every historical peak belongs to the current run.|Gauge decreases are valid; counter reset requires a new baseline; absent fields remain unknown; device identities are matched before subtraction.|Add a disappearing device and shared-inode writeback attribution.|Treat counters, gauges and policy values as distinct types.|A keyed parser must not silently align by line order. Reset and attribution policies are semantic contracts, not formatting choices.
11.c|AFHIJ|Compare a bounded workload with unchanged read-only observations and the object-lifetime ledger. Explain why quota ratio, observed CPU fraction, pressure and retained payload need not agree.|Quota is a guaranteed usage fraction and PSI is exclusive component wait time.|The result preserves inherited/shared scope and gives a causal hypothesis rather than forced equality; missing controllers remain unavailable.|Use a captured throttling history with burst and ancestor limits.|A policy constrains opportunities; it does not prescribe work performed.|Pressure intervals can overlap across resources and tasks. Summing them into a single waiting duration is unjustified.
12.a|BCDFJ|Build matched baseline, bounded entry/exit-hook and supported-pg variants of one existing single-threaded kernel. Verify compile and link flags, recorder exclusion and normal profiling-output lifetime.|Adding one compiler flag instruments every linked object or guarantees a profile file.|Build identities and dependencies are explicit; recursive recorder instrumentation is rejected; unsupported profiling remains separate from the hook result.|Link an uninstrumented helper and identify the missing call boundary.|Inspect compile commands and linked objects, not only the top-level build option.|Instrumentation coverage is a property of the complete build. Runtime termination and buffering affect whether observations are emitted.
12.b|BCDFJ|Implement a LIFO reducer for bounded enter/exit records, including recursion, an unmatched exit, a dropped record and a nonlocal exit. Compute exact inclusive/exclusive time only for complete justified spans.|A truncated trace can be completed by inventing missing zero-duration events.|The parent complete trace gives9/4 for work and5/5 for child; incomplete traces expose unknown attribution rather than exact totals.|Replace recursion with interleaved task IDs and require a stack per context.|A function name is not an invocation identity.|Per-context invocation stacks distinguish recursive frames and concurrency. Nonlocal exits need explicit unwind events or an incomplete-result contract.
12.c|AFHIJ|Measure matched instrumentation overhead and code/stack changes, then use callers with cheap and expensive inputs to falsify uniform child-cost attribution. Keep CPU samples, call counts and off-CPU elapsed time distinct.|Instrumented timing is transparent and average child cost applies equally to all callers.|Report perturbation and the caller-dependent counterexample; no sample count becomes an exact elapsed duration.|Batch calls to reduce bracket overhead and determine whether the new boundary changes the question.|Compare correctness and workload identity before comparing time.|The measurement changes the program; perturbation can alter cache and scheduling behavior. A single subtraction does not remove all effects.
13.a|ACDIJ|Audit the parent's captured DWT prerequisite record in order: exact core/device, trace access, feature presence, ownership, enable, clock and debug state. Reject reading presence fields while the documented trace gate makes them unknown.|A known register address proves an available usable counter.|Unknown, absent, disabled and unowned are distinct results; no actual MMIO or setup change is claimed by the certificate.|Use a device timer with a different clock and state its own access and ownership prerequisites.|Interpret a field only after its validity conditions hold.|Architecture addresses do not prove selected-board behavior. A replacement timer changes the observation model and source requirements.
13.b|ABCFJ|Implement modular delta and checked unit conversion for the supplied32-bit endpoints. Require an independent maximum interval, reject a bound permitting a full wrap and report overhead subtraction only as sensitivity.|Small endpoint difference proves a short elapsed interval.|The bounded case gives64cycles and4000ns; a full-wrap-admitting interval is ambiguous; delta above the declared bound rejects.|Change clock frequency during the interval and derive what can still be inferred.|The endpoint arithmetic and interval-identification argument are separate.|A single frequency conversion assumes a stable clock. Piecewise clock evidence or a different elapsed-time observer is required when that assumption changes.
13.c|AFHIJ|Produce a table separating captured arithmetic, emulated functional execution and dedicated-board cycle observations. Design a GPIO/timer cross-check with stated observer resolution and include a debugger-halt interval.|Emulator timing or halted active-cycle counts establish silicon elapsed time or energy.|No silicon, energy or WCET claim is made without its corresponding observation; halt intervals invalidate simple wall-time conversion.|Transfer the kernel to a cached Cortex-M7 and identify new memory-system assumptions.|Name the physical observable and instrument for each proposed claim.|Cycle count can change with wait states and interrupts even at a fixed clock. A functional emulator result cannot close those physical questions.
14.a|ABHIJ|Write acceptance limits for a reused pipeline covering latency, throughput, retained bytes and traffic; select a minimal justified measurement vector and state why each omitted observable does not decide this requirement.|One benchmark score represents every resource requirement.|Each selected measure has units, scope and a decision threshold; omitted energy or unavailable events are not fabricated.|Add a hard stack bound and determine which prior evidence no longer suffices.|Start from the decision, then choose observables.|An observation is useful when it can distinguish acceptance. More counters do not compensate for a missing binding constraint.
14.b|AFHIJ|Resolve the parent faster-but-larger candidate comparison, then construct a Pareto tradeoff where neither variant dominates in latency and retained storage. Require equivalent correctness and failure contracts before ranking.|The fastest nominal result wins regardless of resource or failure behavior.|B fails the original code/storage constraints; a tradeoff decision cites the requirement and may retain baseline or be inconclusive.|Require a stricter tail deadline while allowing more retained memory.|Check admissibility before optimizing among candidates.|A variant that drops error handling or weakens durability is not an equivalent performance alternative. Changed requirements require a new decision, not a retroactive benchmark claim.
14.c|DFHIJ|Freeze a reproducibility dossier with model proof, finite oracle, actual runtime observations, unavailable capabilities and pending physical evidence in separate records. Introduce a deliberately stale binary hash and reject the mixed result.|A green aggregate report proves all leaf claims or learner mastery.|Every conclusion resolves to its exact artifact and scope; stale/missing observations remain open, and finite tests do not certify absolute mastery.|Ask a second reader to reproduce one changed-condition decision without author explanations.|Trace each claim back to the evidence it actually needs.|Reproducibility and independent assessment are separate from author execution. A delayed unseen task is needed to assess transfer rather than familiarity.
